 First, we need to find the slope \(m\) of the line passing through the points \((-3, 5)\) and \((0, -4)\). The formula for the slope \(m\) is \[
m = \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}
\] Substituting the points \((-3, 5)\) and \((0, -4)\) into the formula, we get \[ \begin{aligned} m &= \frac{-4 - 5}{0 - (-3)} \\ &= \frac{-9}{3} = -3 \end{aligned} \] Next, we can use the point-slope form of the equation of a line, \(y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)\), and substitute one of the points to find the y-intercept \(b\). Using the point \((0, -4)\), the equation becomes \[ y - (-4) = -3(x - 0) \] Simplifying this, we get \[ y + 4 = -3x \] or, \[ y = -3x - 4 \] So the y-intercept \(b\) is \(-4\). The value of \(m + b\) is \(-3 + (-4) = -7\). </think> <answer> -7 </answer>

